# Latin America batch (1492-1886): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"la14920": dict(
 bluf="Genoese navigator Christopher Columbus, funded by Castile, sailed west to reach Asia's trade by a shorter route. He reached the Bahamas and Hispaniola instead and returned convinced he had touched the outskirts of Asia. The landing opened permanent contact between the Americas and the rest of the world: the largest exchange of people, crops, animals and diseases on record, the depopulation of Indigenous societies and the European empires that grew on their ruins.",
 now="The contact opened in 1492 set in motion the movement of people, crops, animals and disease that fixed the populations, languages, religions and racial hierarchies of the Americas over the following three centuries.",
 story="Castile completed the conquest of Granada in January 1492. Genoese navigator Christopher Columbus sailed from Palos in August with three small ships funded by Spanish monarchs Ferdinand and Isabella, landed in the Bahamas on 12 October 1492 and went on to Hispaniola. The Taíno people received him. Within decades disease, forced labour and violence had reduced them to a remnant.",
 why="The Iberian kingdoms were competing for a sea route to Asian trade. Columbus underestimated the size of the earth and so believed Asia lay within reach. Castile, freed by the conquest of Granada, gambled on his plan.",
 trig="Spanish monarchs Ferdinand and Isabella agreed in April 1492 to fund the voyage; Columbus landed in the Bahamas on 12 October.",
 then="Spanish settlement, forced labour and epidemics destroyed the Taíno within decades. Maize and potatoes went east to Europe, Africa and Asia and fed population growth; horses, cattle, wheat and smallpox went west. American silver later financed Spain and flowed on to China.",
 fact="Columbus died still believing he had reached Asia, which is why the Caribbean islands are the 'West Indies'. Tomatoes, potatoes, chocolate and chilli peppers all came from the Americas: Italy had no tomato sauce and India no chilli before this."),
"la14941": dict(
 bluf="Spain and Portugal wanted to settle who owned the lands their navigators were finding before the dispute turned to war. By treaty at Tordesillas in 1494 they divided the world outside Europe along a line 370 leagues west of Cape Verde, with no one who lived there consulted. The line gave Portugal the coast that became Brazil and set the precedent of European powers partitioning other continents on a map.",
 now="The treaty established the practice of distant powers drawing colonial borders without the people inside them, which returned at the Berlin Conference of 1884-85; Brazil's Portuguese language is its direct legacy.",
 story="Columbus's return in 1493 raised the question of who owned the newly found lands. Pope Alexander VI issued bulls that year favouring Spain. Portuguese King John II negotiated directly with Spanish monarchs Ferdinand and Isabella, and on 7 June 1494 at Tordesillas they agreed a line about 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde islands: lands west of it for Spain, east for Portugal. No one asked the people who lived there.",
 why="Columbus's return set off a dispute over who owned newly found lands. Pope Alexander VI's decrees of 1493 favoured Spain, and Portugal negotiated directly to move the line west.",
 then="Brazil's eastern coast fell on Portugal's side, and Portuguese commander Pedro Álvares Cabral claimed it in 1500. France, England and the Dutch rejected the treaty and held that land belonged to whoever occupied it.",
 fact="The line ran 370 leagues west of Cape Verde. Brazil's eastern bulge sticks out past it, which is why Brazil alone in South America speaks Portuguese."),
"la15002": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese commander Pedro Álvares Cabral set out for India by the route Vasco da Gama had opened and claimed the Brazilian coast when his fleet swung west into the Atlantic. Portugal got a colony it held by treaty and showed little interest in for decades. Brazil grew on sugar, gold and enslaved African labour into the largest and richest part of the Portuguese empire and the largest slave society in the Americas.",
 now="Brazil's plantation economy, built from the 1530s on enslaved African labour, produced the largest population of African descent outside Africa and the racial inequalities that run through Brazilian politics today.",
 story="Portugal's second fleet to India left Lisbon in March 1500 under Portuguese commander Pedro Álvares Cabral with 13 ships. Swinging far west into the Atlantic to catch the winds that carried ships round Africa, the fleet sighted the coast of Brazil in April 1500. Cabral claimed the land for Portuguese King Manuel I, stayed about ten days and sent a ship home with the news.",
 why="The route round Africa took ships deep into the South Atlantic, and the coast lay inside the zone the Treaty of Tordesillas had given Portugal.",
 then="Portugal first cut brazilwood for dye, then from the 1530s built sugar plantations in the northeast worked by enslaved Africans; gold strikes in the 1690s drew settlers inland. Brazil received about 40 per cent of all Africans shipped across the Atlantic.",
 fact="Bartolomeu Dias, the first European to round the Cape of Good Hope, sailed with Cabral's fleet and drowned in a storm off that Cape weeks after the Brazilian landfall."),
"la15133": dict(
 bluf="Spanish conquistador Vasco Núñez de Balboa crossed the Isthmus of Panama to find the sea and the gold-rich lands that Indigenous informants had described. He found the sea in September 1513 and claimed it for Spain. The crossing showed that a narrow isthmus separated the Atlantic from an ocean of unknown width and that the Americas lay between Europe and Asia; Panama became Spain's crossroads for Peruvian silver and, four centuries later, the site of a canal.",
 now="The crossing fixed Panama as the land bridge between the oceans: Spanish silver crossed it from the 1530s, and the canal of 1914 uses the same narrow crossing, a chokepoint the United States and China both watch.",
 story="Spanish settlers on the Caribbean coast of Darién had heard from Indigenous people of a sea and rich lands to the south. In September 1513 Spanish conquistador Vasco Núñez de Balboa led about 190 Spaniards and hundreds of Indigenous guides and porters across the Isthmus of Panama. On 25 September he saw the Pacific from a mountain peak. Days later he waded into it and claimed the 'South Sea' for Spain.",
 why="Indigenous people told the settlers of a sea and of rich lands to the south. Balboa needed a success to answer the charges against him at court.",
 then="Portuguese navigator Ferdinand Magellan's expedition crossed the Pacific in 1520-21. Spanish conquistador Francisco Pizarro launched the conquest of the Inca from Panama in the 1530s. Peruvian silver crossed the isthmus on its way to Spain.",
 fact="Balboa was beheaded in 1519 on the orders of Pedrarias Dávila, the governor whose daughter he was betrothed to."),
"d-malinche": dict(
 bluf="Spanish conquistador Hernán Cortés needed to speak with the Aztecs and their rivals; Malintzin, an enslaved Nahua woman given to him in 1519, gave him the means. Through her he negotiated, recruited allies and read Aztec politics, and his force of a few hundred built the coalition that took Tenochtitlan in 1521. She became Mexico's most contested figure of the conquest, read as a traitor by some and as a survivor of enslavement by others.",
 now="Her interpreting made the Spanish-Tlaxcalan alliance of 1519 possible, and the two readings of her, the traitor behind the word 'malinchista' and the enslaved woman surviving, still divide how Mexico tells its founding.",
 story="In March 1519 Spanish conquistador Hernán Cortés defeated the Maya of Potonchán on the Tabasco coast and was given 20 enslaved women. Among them was Malintzin, who spoke Nahuatl and Maya. Working with Spanish castaway Jerónimo de Aguilar, who knew Maya, she translated for Cortés, soon learned Spanish and advised him in his dealings with the Aztecs and their enemies.",
 why="Cortés needed to talk to the Aztecs and their rivals. Malintzin spoke Nahuatl and Maya, learned Spanish within months and understood the region's politics.",
 then="She interpreted at every major encounter, including with Aztec Emperor Moctezuma II in November 1519, and bore Cortés a son, Martín, one of the first prominent mestizos.",
 fact="The Nahua called Cortés 'Malinche' after her; the interpreter's name attached to the man she spoke for."),
"la15194": dict(
 bluf="Spanish conquistador Hernán Cortés landed in Mexico in 1519 for gold, land and standing, in defiance of his governor in Cuba. With about 500 men he recruited the Totonacs and Tlaxcala, peoples who resented Aztec rule, and marched on Tenochtitlan, which fell in 1521. The conquest rested on Indigenous allies and smallpox more than on Spanish arms, and it put the richest state in Mesoamerica under Spain.",
 now="The alliance system Cortés built in 1519 set the pattern of Spanish rule in Mexico: a crown governing through Indigenous nobles and tribute, and a mestizo population that is now the majority.",
 story="Spanish conquistador Hernán Cortés landed on the Gulf coast in 1519 with about 500 men, in defiance of Cuban governor Diego Velázquez, and scuttled his ships so that his men could not turn back. With Malintzin, the Nahua woman who became his interpreter and adviser, he allied with the Totonacs and then with Tlaxcala, longtime enemies of the Aztecs. He marched on Tenochtitlan and entered it in November 1519 as the guest of Aztec Emperor Moctezuma II.",
 why="Cortés wanted gold, land and glory. Subject and rival peoples hated Aztec tribute demands and the taking of captives for sacrifice, and he exploited that hatred.",
 then="Tenochtitlan fell in August 1521 after a siege fought largely by tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, during a smallpox epidemic. Spain built New Spain on the Aztec systems of tribute and labour.",
 fact="Cortés ran his ships aground so that his men could not turn back; the story that he burned them came later."),
"d-noche": dict(
 bluf="The Aztecs of Tenochtitlan rose in June 1520 to drive out Spanish conquistador Hernán Cortés and his Tlaxcalan allies after a massacre at a festival. They drove the invaders from the city on the night of 30 June, killing hundreds of Spaniards and many more Tlaxcalans. The rising showed the Spanish could be beaten on Aztec ground; Cortés survived because Tlaxcala stayed loyal, and the smallpox that reached the city that autumn tilted the balance back.",
 now="The defeat made the conquest a war of Indigenous coalitions: Cortés rebuilt his force from Tlaxcala, and the smallpox that followed killed the Aztec leadership that had won, which settled the siege of 1521.",
 story="Spanish captain Pedro de Alvarado's men killed Aztec nobles at the festival of Toxcatl in May 1520 while Cortés was away on the coast. The city rose. Aztec Emperor Moctezuma II died in Spanish custody. On the night of 30 June 1520 Cortés tried to escape along the Tlacopan causeway; hundreds of Spaniards and many more Tlaxcalan allies died, some weighed down by the gold they carried.",
 why="Alvarado's massacre of Aztec nobles at Toxcatl provoked the rising. Moctezuma's death in Spanish custody left the Spaniards without a hostage to bargain with.",
 then="Cortés retreated to Tlaxcala, rebuilt with reinforcements and Indigenous allies and had brigantines built for a siege of the lake city. Smallpox killed Moctezuma's successor, Aztec Emperor Cuitláhuac, in late 1520 along with many of the city's defenders.",
 fact="In 2021 Mexico City renamed the tree linked to the night from the 'Noche Triste' (sad night) to the 'Noche Victoriosa' (victorious night)."),
"la15215": dict(
 bluf="Spanish conquistador Hernán Cortés and tens of thousands of Tlaxcalan and other Indigenous allies besieged Tenochtitlan to destroy the Aztec state. The city fell on 13 August 1521 after 75 days, its defenders weakened by smallpox and starvation. The Aztec Empire ended, central Mexico became the core of Spain's American empire, and the largest city in the Americas was destroyed block by block.",
 now="Spain ruled central Mexico through the Aztec tribute and labour systems from 1521, and the collision of Indigenous and Spanish worlds produced the mestizo majority, the Spanish language and the Catholic faith of modern Mexico.",
 story="Spanish conquistador Hernán Cortés returned to the Valley of Mexico in 1521 with Spanish reinforcements, tens of thousands of Tlaxcalan and other Indigenous allies and brigantines built to control the lake. Smallpox had already killed large numbers, including Aztec Emperor Cuitláhuac. After a 75-day siege the last emperor, Cuauhtémoc, was captured on 13 August 1521, and the city was destroyed block by block.",
 why="Aztec rule rested on tribute and fear rather than integration, so subject and rival peoples joined the invaders. Smallpox, to which the peoples of the Americas had no immunity, devastated the defenders.",
 then="Spain built Mexico City on the ruins and ran New Spain through the existing Indigenous nobility and tribute systems. Disease and forced labour cut central Mexico's Indigenous population by 80 to 90 per cent within a century, on most estimates. Tlaxcala kept privileges as a reward for its alliance.",
 fact="Tenochtitlan, built on islands in a lake, had perhaps 200,000 people, more than any city in Spain. Mexico City still sinks every year because it sits on the old lakebed."),
"d-incacivil": dict(
 bluf="Inca princes Atahualpa and Huáscar, sons of Emperor Huayna Capac, fought for the throne after their father's death around 1527. Atahualpa's generals won in 1532 and captured Huáscar near Cusco. The war split the Inca state months before Spanish conquistador Francisco Pizarro reached Cajamarca, which let him play the factions against each other and drew Andean peoples who resented the victors to the newcomers' side.",
 now="The civil war left the empire in 1532 with a captive rival emperor, a purged nobility and resentful subject peoples, which together with smallpox is the main reason a few hundred Spaniards could seize it.",
 story="Inca Emperor Huayna Capac died around 1527, probably of smallpox. His sons Huáscar, ruling in Cusco, and Atahualpa, commanding the armies in the north, fought for the throne. In 1532 Atahualpa's generals Quizquiz and Chalcuchima defeated and captured Huáscar near Cusco, months before Spanish conquistador Francisco Pizarro reached Cajamarca.",
 why="Inca succession had no fixed rule. An epidemic, probably smallpox spreading ahead of the Spanish, killed Huayna Capac and his chosen heir around 1527.",
 then="Huáscar's supporters in Cusco and conquered peoples such as the Cañari and Chachapoya resented the victors, and some later joined the Spanish. Atahualpa had Huáscar killed in 1533 while himself a Spanish prisoner.",
 fact="The heir Huayna Capac named, Ninan Cuyochi, died in the same epidemic within days of his father, which is what left the throne to be fought over."),
"la15326": dict(
 bluf="Spanish conquistador Francisco Pizarro went to Cajamarca with about 170 men to seize Inca Emperor Atahualpa and with him the empire's command. The ambush of 16 November 1532 succeeded; Atahualpa paid a room of gold and silver for his release and was executed anyway in 1533. The capture decapitated the largest state in the Americas, opened the Andes to Spanish conquest and put the silver found at Potosí in 1545 in Spain's hands to finance its wars and knit together a world economy.",
 now="Spanish rule from 1532 fixed the Andes' divide between a Spanish-speaking coast and a Quechua-speaking highland that still structures politics in Peru, Bolivia and Ecuador, where millions speak Quechua and Indigenous movements frame their claims in terms of the conquest.",
 story="The Inca state had just come through a civil war and a smallpox epidemic. On 16 November 1532 at Cajamarca about 170 Spaniards under Spanish conquistador Francisco Pizarro ambushed Inca Emperor Atahualpa's unarmed retinue with guns, horses and steel and captured him. He paid a room filled with gold and silver for his release. Pizarro executed him in July 1533 and marched on Cusco with Indigenous allies.",
 why="Smallpox and a civil war had weakened the empire. Its centralised structure meant that capturing the ruler paralysed command.",
 trig="Atahualpa agreed to meet the Spaniards in Cajamarca's square, confident in the army of tens of thousands camped nearby.",
 then="Spain installed puppet Inca rulers, faced a great rebellion under Manco Inca in 1536 and fought wars among the conquerors themselves; Pizarro was assassinated in Lima in 1541. An Inca state held out in Vilcabamba until 1572. Spain founded Lima in 1535 and built the Viceroyalty of Peru on Andean labour systems.",
 fact="Atahualpa offered to fill a room once with gold and twice with silver as ransom. He paid it, and the Spanish executed him anyway."),
"d-lima": dict(
 bluf="Spanish conquistador Francisco Pizarro founded a capital on the coast where ships from Panama and Spain could reach it, rather than at Cusco high in the Andes. Lima became the seat of the Viceroyalty of Peru, which governed most of Spanish South America. Peru's centre of power moved from the Indigenous highlands to a Spanish coastal city, a divide the country still carries.",
 now="Peru's sharp divide between a dominant coastal capital and the Andean and Amazonian interior, visible in its election maps, goes back to this choice.",
 story="On 18 January 1535 Spanish conquistador Francisco Pizarro founded the Ciudad de los Reyes on the Rímac River a few miles from the sea. It became the capital of the Viceroyalty of Peru, created in 1542, which governed most of Spanish South America.",
 why="A coastal capital linked the colony to Panama and Spain. Cusco lay high in the Andes and close to Inca resistance.",
 then="Lima became home to the viceroy, the Inquisition from 1570, the merchants who controlled silver exports and the University of San Marcos, founded in 1551, one of the oldest in the Americas.",
 fact="Pizarro was murdered in his own palace in Lima in 1541 by followers of his rival Diego de Almagro."),
"la15457": dict(
 bluf="Spain wanted revenue, and in 1545 its colonists found the richest silver deposit in the world at Potosí. The crown took a fifth of the output and compelled Andean labour to dig the rest, which devastated Indigenous communities. The silver financed Spain's wars, fed inflation across Europe and flowed on to China, creating the first trade that ran around the globe.",
 now="The Spanish silver peso, struck from Potosí's output, served as the first global currency from the 1570s and was the model for the US dollar; Bolivia's poverty, with the hill mined out, is the standard case of a resource enriching everyone except its owners.",
 story="In 1545 silver was found at the Cerro Rico ('rich hill') in the Andes of today's Bolivia. Potosí grew to well over 100,000 people, one of the largest cities in the Americas. From the 1570s Peruvian Viceroy Francisco de Toledo organised the mita, a forced labour draft that sent Indigenous men to the mines, and refining with mercury greatly increased output.",
 why="Spain's hunger for revenue and China's demand for silver made the mine worth any cost. The colonial state could compel Andean labour to work it.",
 then="Silver paid for Spain's wars, fed inflation across Europe (the 'price revolution') and left Spain dependent on bullion rather than manufacturing. Through the Manila galleons from 1565 much of it ended up in Ming China, which had shifted its taxes to silver.",
 fact="Spanish still has the phrase 'vale un Potosí' (worth a Potosí) for something priceless."),
"la15658": dict(
 bluf="Spain wanted a way to reach Asian markets from its American colonies, and in 1565 Spanish navigator Andrés de Urdaneta found the return route across the Pacific that made a regular trade possible. For 250 years galleons carried American silver from Acapulco to Manila and brought back Chinese silk and porcelain. The route completed the first global trade network and tied Ming China's economy to Spanish American mines.",
 now="The galleon trade linked American silver to Chinese demand from 1565 to 1815, the first exchange to run around the globe; the cultural ties between Mexico and the Philippines and the Chinese merchant communities of Southeast Asia date from it.",
 story="Spain had reached the Philippines but could not sail back east against the trade winds. In 1565 Spanish navigator Andrés de Urdaneta found the eastward route across the Pacific, using the westerly winds of the north Pacific. For 250 years galleons carried Mexican and Peruvian silver from Acapulco to Manila to buy Chinese silk and porcelain, and sailed back once or twice a year.",
 why="China's economy had shifted to silver for taxes and trade, so silver was worth far more there than in Europe. Spain needed a way to reach Asian markets from its American colonies.",
 then="Silver flowed to China for 250 years. American crops such as maize, sweet potatoes and chillies spread to Asia and helped China's population grow. Chinese merchants settled in Manila.",
 fact="For 250 years one or two ships a year, among the largest afloat, crossed the Pacific. Chinese porcelain shards still turn up in Mexican digs."),
"la15729": dict(
 bluf="Peruvian Viceroy Francisco de Toledo set out to end the last independent Inca state and tighten Spain's hold on Andean labour. His army took Vilcabamba in 1572 and captured Inca ruler Túpac Amaru, whom he had beheaded in Cusco. The conquest of the Andes was complete, and Toledo reorganised Andean society to extract silver and tribute.",
 now="Toledo's reorganisation after 1572, the planned towns and the formalised mita, set the terms of Andean labour until independence, and Túpac Amaru's name became the banner of resistance from the rebellion of 1780 onward.",
 story="For 35 years after the conquest, Inca rulers held out in the remote forest state of Vilcabamba. In 1572 Peruvian Viceroy Francisco de Toledo sent an army, which captured the last Inca ruler, Túpac Amaru. Toledo had him beheaded in Cusco's main square in September 1572.",
 why="Toledo wanted the last independent Inca state gone and Spanish control over Andean labour tightened.",
 then="Toledo forced Indigenous people into new planned towns (reducciones), formalised the mita labour draft for Potosí and cracked down on the Inca nobility. Resistance went underground and resurfaced in later rebellions.",
 fact="Túpac Amaru's name was taken up by the rebellion of 1780, by guerrilla movements in Peru and Uruguay and by the rapper Tupac Shakur."),
"la169310": dict(
 bluf="Bandeirantes from São Paulo searched the interior for captives and precious metals and found gold in Minas Gerais in the 1690s. The rush drew tens of thousands of Portuguese settlers and enslaved Africans inland and made Minas Gerais the colony's richest region. Brazil's centre of gravity moved south and inland, demand for enslaved Africans rose, and much of the gold flowed through Portugal to Britain, where it helped finance commercial expansion.",
 now="The gold moved Brazil's capital to Rio de Janeiro in 1763 and, through the Methuen Treaty of 1703, made Portugal a market for British cloth paid in bullion; Minas Gerais remains a mining state, and the dam collapses at Mariana (2015) and Brumadinho (2019) show the industry's continuing costs.",
 story="In the 1690s bandeirantes, slave-raiding explorers from São Paulo, found gold in the hills of Minas Gerais. Tens of thousands of Portuguese settlers and enslaved Africans poured in. Towns such as Ouro Preto grew rich on the output.",
 why="Bandeirantes searching the interior for captives and precious metals struck gold in Minas Gerais. Portugal, short of revenue, taxed the output at a fifth and policed the roads.",
 then="Minas Gerais became Brazil's richest region, and the capital moved from Salvador to Rio de Janeiro in 1763, the port for the mines. Under the Methuen Treaty of 1703 Portugal bought British cloth and paid the difference in Brazilian gold. Diamonds were found in the same region in the 1720s.",
 fact="Ouro Preto's baroque churches were carved by Aleijadinho, the son of a Portuguese architect and an enslaved African woman."),
"la178011": dict(
 bluf="José Gabriel Condorcanqui, a kuraka of Inca descent who took the name Túpac Amaru II, rose in November 1780 to end abusive officials, forced purchases and the mita. His rebellion, the largest Indigenous uprising in colonial Spanish America, was crushed within months and he was executed in 1781. The suppression left Creole elites afraid of Indigenous mobilisation, which shaped Peru's cautious, elite-led road to independence.",
 now="The fear the rebellion left among Peru's Creoles kept them loyal to Spain longer than any other mainland elite, so independence came to Peru from outside in 1821-24 and left the Indigenous majority outside politics.",
 story="In November 1780 José Gabriel Condorcanqui, a wealthy mule-train owner and kuraka of Inca descent, seized and executed the Spanish corregidor Antonio de Arriaga. Calling himself Túpac Amaru II, he led tens of thousands of Indigenous and mestizo rebels against abusive officials and forced labour.",
 why="Crushing taxes came first. Forced purchases of goods from officials and the mita labour draft added to them.",
 then="He was captured and executed in Cusco in May 1781, and Spain banned Inca dress, symbols and titles. The rebellion continued in Upper Peru (Bolivia) under Aymara leader Túpac Katari, who besieged La Paz in 1781.",
 fact="Túpac Amaru II was executed in the same Cusco square where Túpac Amaru I had been beheaded in 1572; the order was to tear him apart with four horses, and when that failed he was beheaded."),
"la179112": dict(
 bluf="The enslaved of Saint-Domingue rose in August 1791 to end a sugar slavery that killed them faster than planters could import replacements. Under Haitian general Toussaint Louverture and then Jean-Jacques Dessalines they beat Spanish, British and finally French armies and declared independence in 1804. The only slave revolt on record to found a state destroyed the world's most profitable colony, terrified every slaveholding society and pushed Napoleon to sell Louisiana to the United States.",
 now="Haiti paid France's indemnity and the loans taken to service it until 1947, which drained its economy for 122 years; the revolution remains the reference point for Black freedom and anti-colonial movements.",
 story="Saint-Domingue produced much of the world's sugar and coffee with the labour of about 500,000 enslaved Africans, worked so hard that planters constantly imported more. In August 1791 the enslaved of the northern plain rose. Under Haitian general Toussaint Louverture and later Jean-Jacques Dessalines they fought off Spanish and British invasions and, in 1802-03, the army French First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte sent to retake the colony.",
 why="Sugar slavery of exceptional brutality came first. Free people of colour were demanding equal rights. The French Revolution's declarations of liberty divided the colony's whites and inspired the enslaved.",
 trig="A ceremony at Bois Caïman and coordinated uprisings across the northern plain in August 1791.",
 then="France abolished slavery in 1794, and Haiti declared independence on 1 January 1804. French ruler Napoleon Bonaparte's defeat there led him to sell Louisiana in 1803. Haiti was isolated: the United States withheld recognition until 1862, and France demanded an indemnity of 150 million francs in 1825 in return for recognising it.",
 fact="Napoleon's failure to retake Haiti is why he gave up on America and sold Louisiana."),
"d-toussaint": dict(
 bluf="French First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte sent an army under his brother-in-law, French general Charles Leclerc, to retake Saint-Domingue, remove Haitian general Toussaint Louverture and restore slavery. Leclerc seized Toussaint by treachery in June 1802 and shipped him to France, where he died in prison. The arrest backfired: news that France had restored slavery in Guadeloupe convinced the Haitian officers who had submitted to fight on to full independence.",
 now="The arrest removed the one leader who had kept Saint-Domingue inside the French empire, and the restoration of slavery that followed it turned a colonial war into a war of independence, won in 1803.",
 story="French First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte sent an army of tens of thousands under French general Charles Leclerc to retake Saint-Domingue in early 1802. Haitian general Toussaint Louverture submitted in May. On 7 June 1802 he was arrested at a meeting he had been invited to, deported to France and held in the fortress of Joux in the Jura mountains, where he died in April 1803.",
 why="Napoleon wanted French control restored and, as became clear within months, slavery with it. Leclerc judged Toussaint too dangerous to leave at liberty.",
 then="France restored slavery in Guadeloupe in 1802, and when the news reached Saint-Domingue the Haitian officers under Jean-Jacques Dessalines rose again in October. Leclerc died of yellow fever in November 1802.",
 fact="Boarding the ship, Toussaint is reported to have said that France had cut down only the trunk of the tree of liberty in Saint-Domingue, and that it would grow back from roots that were many and deep."),
"d-vertieres": dict(
 bluf="Haitian general Jean-Jacques Dessalines attacked the last French positions around Cap-Français to finish the war for independence. On 18 November 1803 his army broke the French line at Vertières, and French general Donatien de Rochambeau agreed to evacuate. Napoleon's attempt to retake Saint-Domingue and restore slavery was over, and Haiti declared independence six weeks later, the first state born of a slave revolt.",
 now="Vertières made Haiti the second independent state in the Americas and the first founded by the formerly enslaved, a precedent slaveholding powers answered with isolation and France with an indemnity.",
 story="Yellow fever had killed much of the French army, and the renewal of war with Britain in May 1803 cut it off from reinforcement. On 18 November 1803 Haitian general Jean-Jacques Dessalines's army attacked the French positions at Vertières outside Cap-Français; Haitian officer François Capois led repeated charges under fire. French general Donatien de Rochambeau agreed to evacuate. Haiti declared independence on 1 January 1804.",
 why="Yellow fever had destroyed much of the French army. Renewed war with Britain in 1803 cut off reinforcements and brought a British blockade.",
 then="Haiti became the first state founded after a successful revolt by enslaved people. Rochambeau surrendered to the British blockading squadron rather than to Dessalines.",
 fact="Capois's horse was shot from under him during the assault, and Rochambeau is said to have ordered a pause in the firing to salute his courage."),
"la180413": dict(
 bluf="Haitian general Jean-Jacques Dessalines declared independence on 1 January 1804 to make the defeat of France permanent and slavery impossible to restore. Haiti became the first Black republic and the first state to abolish slavery for good. The great powers answered with isolation, and France with an indemnity in 1825 that Haiti paid for over a century, which shows how threatening a free Black state was to the slaveholding world.",
 now="The indemnity of 1825 and the loans taken to pay it drained Haiti until 1947, one reading of the country's poverty; in 2025 French President Emmanuel Macron called the indemnity an injustice and set up a joint commission to study it.",
 story="On 1 January 1804 at Gonaïves, Haitian general Jean-Jacques Dessalines declared the independence of Haiti, taking back the island's Taíno name. He abolished slavery for good. In October 1804 he had himself crowned Emperor Jacques I.",
 why="Haitian armies had defeated Napoleon's attempt to restore French rule and slavery. Only a sovereign state could guarantee that no planter returned.",
 then="Dessalines ordered the killing of most of the remaining French whites in early 1804. He was assassinated in October 1806 and the country split between north and south. France recognised Haiti in 1825 in return for a 150 million franc indemnity, which Haiti paid off, with the loans raised to meet it, over more than a century.",
 fact="Haiti's constitution of 1805 declared every citizen, whatever their colour, to be 'Black'."),
"la180814": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese Prince Regent João moved the court to Brazil to keep the dynasty out of Napoleon's hands when the French army marched on Lisbon in November 1807. He got away under British escort with thousands of courtiers and ruled the empire from Rio de Janeiro. Brazil rose from colony to kingdom, and when independence came in 1822 it came as one unified monarchy under João's son rather than the fragmentation of Spanish America.",
 now="Thirteen years of government from Rio gave Brazil the institutions of a state before independence, which is why it separated from Portugal in 1822 as one monarchy under a Portuguese prince rather than breaking into republics as Spanish America did.",
 story="In November 1807, as French general Jean-Andoche Junot's army approached Lisbon, Portuguese Prince Regent João, the royal family and thousands of courtiers sailed for Brazil under British naval escort. Rio de Janeiro became the capital of the Portuguese empire in 1808. João opened Brazil's ports to friendly nations, ending the colonial trade monopoly.",
 why="Napoleon invaded Portugal for refusing to join his blockade of Britain. Britain preferred the dynasty in Brazil, where its trade could follow.",
 then="Brazil gained a printing press, a bank, schools and a naval academy, and was raised to a kingdom equal with Portugal in 1815. When King João VI returned to Portugal in 1821, his son Pedro stayed and declared Brazilian independence in 1822.",
 fact="It is the only time a European empire was ruled from one of its colonies."),
"la181015": dict(
 bluf="Mexican parish priest Miguel Hidalgo called the Indigenous and mestizo poor of Dolores to rise against Spanish rule on 16 September 1810. His army of tens of thousands sacked Guanajuato and reached the outskirts of Mexico City before it broke; he was captured and shot in 1811. The violence of the rising frightened Creole elites, which is why independence, when it came in 1821, was made by conservatives.",
 now="Hidalgo's rising fixed the split in Mexican independence between a popular revolt and the conservative settlement of 1821; Mexico celebrates the first and was founded by the second, a tension that ran through its politics to 1910.",
 story="Spanish King Ferdinand VII was Napoleon's prisoner and Spain's authority in Mexico was in dispute. On 16 September 1810 Mexican parish priest Miguel Hidalgo rang the church bell at Dolores and called on Indigenous and mestizo Mexicans to rise. His followers took Guanajuato and killed its Spanish defenders. He was captured and executed in 1811.",
 why="Napoleon's removal of Spain's king in 1808 created a crisis of authority. Creoles resented Spanish-born officials. Indigenous and mestizo grievances over land and tribute supplied the mass following.",
 then="The war lasted 11 years. Independence came in 1821 under the conservative officer Agustín de Iturbide, who briefly made himself emperor.",
 fact="Mexico's Independence Day is 16 September, the anniversary of Hidalgo's cry; Cinco de Mayo marks a battle of 1862."),
"la181616": dict(
 bluf="The delegates at Tucumán wanted to make permanent the self-government the Río de la Plata had exercised since 1810 and to give Argentine general José de San Martín a state to fight for. They declared independence on 9 July 1816, and San Martín's crossing of the Andes the next year carried the war to Chile and Peru. The break with Spain was complete, and decades of civil war between Buenos Aires and the provinces followed, fixing Argentina's divide between port and interior.",
 now="The rivalry between Buenos Aires and the provinces, fought out from 1816 to the constitution of 1853, still shapes Argentina's federal politics.",
 story="Local juntas had governed the Río de la Plata since May 1810 in the name of a captive Spanish king. On 9 July 1816 delegates of the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata met at Tucumán and declared independence from Spain. In January 1817 Argentine general José de San Martín led an army across the Andes to free Chile and then Peru.",
 why="Spain's crisis under Napoleon had let local juntas govern since 1810. Spanish King Ferdinand VII's restoration in 1814 and his reconquest of other colonies made a formal break urgent.",
 then="Argentina fought civil wars for decades between Buenos Aires, which wanted a strong centre, and provincial strongmen who wanted federalism. A lasting constitution came only in 1853.",
 fact="San Martín crossed the Andes with about 5,000 men through passes above 3,500 metres and beat the royalists at Chacabuco on 12 February 1817."),
"la181917": dict(
 bluf="Venezuelan general Simón Bolívar, beaten repeatedly in Venezuela, marched his army across the Llanos and over the Andes to strike Spain where it was weak, in New Granada. He won at Boyacá on 7 August 1819 and took Bogotá three days later. New Granada was freed, the tide turned in northern South America, and Bolívar went on to create Gran Colombia and lead the campaigns that freed five countries.",
 now="Bolívar's victories from 1819 founded Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia; Venezuela's official name and Hugo Chávez's 'Bolivarian revolution' from 1999 show his continuing political use.",
 story="Spain's authority had collapsed after Napoleon invaded it in 1808, and its reconquest after 1815 had turned many Creoles against it. In 1819 Venezuelan general Simón Bolívar led his army across the flooded plains of the Llanos and over the freezing Andes at the Páramo de Pisba into New Granada. On 7 August at the bridge of Boyacá he defeated the Spanish army under Spanish colonel José María Barreiro. Bogotá fell three days later.",
 why="Spain's authority had collapsed after Napoleon invaded it in 1808 and Creole elites wanted self-rule. Spain's harsh reconquest after 1815 had turned many against it. Bolívar needed a new strategy after repeated defeats in Venezuela.",
 then="He created Gran Colombia in December 1819 and helped free Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia. Independence was led by Creole elites, and the colonial social hierarchy, including the exclusion of Indigenous people, largely remained.",
 fact="Bolivia is named after Bolívar, and so is Venezuela's currency, the bolívar."),
"la182118": dict(
 bluf="Mexico's conservative Creoles, alarmed by the liberal revolution in Spain of 1820, decided that independence was safer for Church and army privileges than rule from Madrid. Royalist officer Agustín de Iturbide joined insurgent leader Vicente Guerrero in the Plan of Iguala, and Spain's last viceroy accepted it in 1821. Mexico was independent on terms that preserved the colonial elite, with a vast, divided territory and fragile institutions, and it lost half of that territory to the United States within a generation.",
 now="Mexico became Latin America's largest Spanish-speaking nation. Arguments over the role of the Church, the army and the regions shaped its politics through the revolution of 1910.",
 story="Royalist officer Agustín de Iturbide, who had fought Mexico's rebels for a decade, switched sides in 1821 and joined insurgent leader Vicente Guerrero. Their Plan of Iguala promised independence, Catholicism and equality between Creoles and Spaniards. Spanish viceroy Juan O'Donojú accepted it in August 1821, and Iturbide entered Mexico City in September. Iturbide made himself emperor in 1822 and was overthrown a year later.",
 why="Conservative Creole elites, alarmed by a liberal revolution in Spain that threatened Church and army privileges, decided independence was safer than rule from Madrid.",
 trig="Spain's liberal revolution of 1820, which restored its 1812 constitution.",
 then="Mexico's first decades brought constant coups and conflict between centralists and federalists, liberals and conservatives. Texas broke away in 1836, and the United States took the north in 1848.",
 fact="Iturbide was shot in 1824 when he returned from exile, unaware that Congress had declared him an outlaw."),
"la182219": dict(
 bluf="Brazilian elites and Prince Regent Pedro wanted to keep the autonomy Brazil had gained when Portugal's parliament ordered it back to colonial status. Pedro refused the recall, declared independence on 7 September 1822 and was crowned emperor. Brazil stayed united under a monarchy and avoided the wars and fragmentation of Spanish America; it also kept its plantation elite and slavery, which it abolished last in the Americas, in 1888.",
 now="Independence under a prince kept Brazil one state and one monarchy until 1889 and left the plantation elite in charge, which is why slavery lasted there until 1888, later than anywhere else in the Americas.",
 story="Portugal's parliament ordered Brazil back to colonial status in 1821 and recalled Prince Regent Pedro. Advised by Brazilian statesman José Bonifácio de Andrada, he refused in January 1822. On 7 September 1822, by the Ipiranga stream near São Paulo, he declared 'Independence or death!'. He was crowned Emperor Pedro I that December.",
 why="Portugal's parliament tried to return Brazil to colonial status after the royal court went home. Brazilian elites rallied behind Prince Pedro to keep their autonomy.",
 then="Brazil stayed united under a monarchy until 1889 and kept slavery until 1888, the last country in the Americas to abolish it. Portugal recognised independence in 1825 in return for a payment.",
 fact="Pedro I later abdicated, returned to Europe and fought a civil war to put his daughter Maria II on the Portuguese throne."),
"la182420": dict(
 bluf="Venezuelan general Simón Bolívar and his lieutenant, Venezuelan general Antonio José de Sucre, set out to destroy the last royalist army in South America, which held the Peruvian highlands. Sucre beat it at Ayacucho on 9 December 1824 and took the viceroy prisoner. Spain's mainland American empire was over, leaving it Cuba and Puerto Rico, and the new republics inherited colonial borders, elites and inequalities.",
 now="Ayacucho closed Spain's mainland empire in 1824; the republics that replaced it kept the colonial borders, elites and inequalities, which still shape the region's politics.",
 story="On 9 December 1824, high in the Peruvian Andes, Venezuelan general Antonio José de Sucre's patriot army of about 6,000 defeated the larger royalist army of the last Spanish viceroy, José de La Serna, at Ayacucho. The viceroy was wounded and captured and his army surrendered.",
 why="Spain's last army in South America held the Peruvian highlands. Bolívar and Sucre needed it destroyed to secure the independence of the whole continent.",
 then="Spain's mainland American empire was over. Upper Peru became Bolivia in 1825, named after Bolívar, with Sucre as its first president.",
 fact="The capitulation signed at Ayacucho let the Spanish officers sail home at Peru's expense; a Spanish garrison held out at Callao until January 1826."),
"la183021": dict(
 bluf="Venezuelan general Simón Bolívar had built Gran Colombia to hold Colombia, Venezuela, Ecuador and Panama in one republic strong enough to stand beside the United States. Regional elites resented rule from Bogotá, and in 1830 Venezuela under general José Antonio Páez and then Ecuador under general Juan José Flores seceded; Bolívar resigned and died that December. His large South American republic was finished, and the continent settled into many states run by regional elites and caudillos.",
 now="The breakup of 1830 fixed Latin America as a set of mid-sized states rather than one union, a fragmentation that left the region weaker beside the United States; Chávez revived 'Bolivarian' unity as a political programme after 1999.",
 story="Gran Colombia, which Venezuelan general Simón Bolívar had built from today's Colombia, Venezuela, Ecuador and Panama, came apart in 1830. Venezuela under general José Antonio Páez seceded first, then Ecuador under general Juan José Flores. Bolívar resigned in May 1830 and died of tuberculosis on 17 December 1830 at Santa Marta, having written that he had 'ploughed the sea'.",
 why="Regional elites resented rule from Bogotá. Bolívar's attempt at a strong central government, including his dictatorship of 1828, made enemies among liberals.",
 then="Latin America stayed divided into many states, often ruled by regional strongmen. Panama remained part of Colombia until 1903.",
 fact="Bolívar survived an assassination attempt in Bogotá in September 1828 by climbing out of a window while his companion Manuela Sáenz held off the attackers."),
"d-baptistwar": dict(
 bluf="Baptist deacon Samuel Sharpe organised the enslaved of western Jamaica to withhold their labour after Christmas 1831 until they were paid wages, on the belief that freedom had already been granted in London. The strike became the largest slave rising in the British Caribbean and was crushed in weeks, with about 200 rebels killed and over 300 executed, Sharpe among them. The rising and its suppression convinced Parliament that slavery could be held only by constant force, and the Abolition Act followed in 1833.",
 now="The rising, and the planters' reprisals against Baptist missionaries that followed, shifted British opinion in 1832 and brought the Abolition Act of 1833, which freed some 800,000 people across the empire from 1834.",
 story="Enslaved Jamaicans believed that freedom had been granted in London and withheld by the planters. Baptist deacon Samuel Sharpe planned a peaceful strike after Christmas 1831, and tens of thousands joined. It turned into a revolt across the western parishes. Fourteen white people and about 200 rebels died in the fighting, and over 300 more were executed afterwards, Sharpe among them, in May 1832.",
 why="Rumours that freedom had already been granted in London came first. Conditions on the sugar estates supplied the grievance.",
 then="Parliament passed the Slavery Abolition Act in 1833. Slave owners received £20 million in compensation; the formerly enslaved got nothing and had to work years of unpaid 'apprenticeship', which ended in 1838.",
 fact="Sharpe is reported to have said on the gallows that he would rather die than live in slavery; he is a National Hero of Jamaica and appears on the $50 note."),
"d-beagle": dict(
 bluf="British naval captain Robert FitzRoy wanted a gentleman naturalist for a survey of South America's coasts, and 22-year-old Charles Darwin took the place. Five years of fossils, geology and island species gave Darwin the observations from which he built the theory of evolution by natural selection. The voyage shows how imperial survey work produced science, and it produced the idea that reorganised biology.",
 now="Darwin's observations of 1831-36 led to 'On the Origin of Species' in 1859, which made common descent the organising idea of biology; the Galápagos remain its living laboratory and a test of conservation.",
 story="HMS Beagle left Plymouth on 27 December 1831 under British naval captain Robert FitzRoy to chart South America's coasts. English naturalist Charles Darwin, aged 22, collected fossils and specimens along the way. In the Galápagos in 1835 he noticed that mockingbirds differed from island to island. The ship returned in October 1836.",
 why="FitzRoy wanted a gentleman companion and naturalist for the long voyage. Darwin's Cambridge tutor John Stevens Henslow recommended him.",
 then="Darwin opened his notebooks on the 'transmutation' of species in 1837 and published 'On the Origin of Species' in 1859.",
 fact="FitzRoy nearly rejected Darwin because of the shape of his nose, which he read as a sign of weak character."),
"la184822": dict(
 bluf="The United States wanted California and the Southwest and had seized them by force; Mexico, with US troops in its capital, needed peace on any terms it could get. The treaty signed on 2 February 1848 transferred about half of Mexico's territory for $15 million. It fixed most of the modern US-Mexico border and made some 80,000 Mexicans US residents overnight, the origin of the Mexican-American population of the Southwest.",
 now="The treaty's land and citizenship clauses created the Mexican-American population of the Southwest and the border they live along, and the loss of half the national territory has shaped Mexican attitudes to the United States ever since.",
 story="US troops occupied Mexico City in September 1847. Signed on 2 February 1848 at Guadalupe Hidalgo near Mexico City by US envoy Nicholas Trist and Mexican commissioners, the treaty gave the United States about half of Mexico's territory, including California, Nevada, Utah and most of Arizona and New Mexico, for $15 million. Mexicans living there could become US citizens.",
 why="US troops occupied Mexico City, and Mexico, divided and bankrupt, could only make peace on American terms. US President James K. Polk had gone to war for California.",
 then="About 80,000 Mexicans became US residents; their property rights, though promised, were often lost in later land disputes. The Gadsden Purchase of 1853 added the last strip of the border.",
 fact="Trist negotiated the treaty after Polk had recalled him; he ignored the recall, and Polk sent the treaty to the Senate anyway. 'We didn't cross the border, the border crossed us' is a saying in the US Southwest."),
"la186223": dict(
 bluf="French Emperor Napoleon III invaded Mexico to install a Catholic monarchy under French protection while the United States was absorbed by its civil war. His army lost at Puebla on 5 May 1862, took Mexico City in 1863 and placed Austrian archduke Maximilian on a throne that fell in 1867. The scheme failed against Mexican resistance and US pressure, and it strengthened Mexican nationalism and the republic under President Benito Juárez.",
 now="The collapse of the French-backed empire in 1867 discredited monarchy in Mexico for good and fixed the liberal republic under Juárez, with the Church's power limited by the reform laws.",
 story="In 1861 Mexico under President Benito Juárez suspended payments on its foreign debts. Britain, France and Spain landed troops at Veracruz; Britain and Spain soon withdrew, but French Emperor Napoleon III's forces marched inland, seeking a Catholic monarchy friendly to France. Mexican forces under general Ignacio Zaragoza beat the French at Puebla on 5 May 1862. France took Mexico City in June 1863.",
 why="Mexico suspended its debt payments. Napoleon III wanted a French-backed empire while the United States was busy with its civil war, and Mexican conservatives beaten in the Reform War invited him.",
 then="France took Mexico City in 1863 and made Austrian archduke Maximilian emperor in 1864, but faced continuing guerrilla resistance and the hostility of the United States once its civil war ended in 1865.",
 fact="Cinco de Mayo, the 1862 victory at Puebla, is now celebrated more in the United States than in Mexico."),
"la186424": dict(
 bluf="Paraguayan President Francisco Solano López intervened in Uruguay's civil war in 1864 to check Brazil and then invaded Brazilian and Argentine territory. Brazil, Argentina and Uruguay allied against him and fought until he was killed in 1870. Paraguay lost a large share of its population and territory in the deadliest war in Latin American history, and Brazil and Argentina were confirmed as the region's powers.",
 now="The losses of 1864-70, perhaps over half of Paraguay's people, and the territory ceded to Brazil and Argentina fixed Paraguay's standing as the region's weak state and its wariness of its larger neighbours.",
 story="In 1864 Paraguayan President Francisco Solano López intervened in a Uruguayan civil war against Brazil's ally and invaded Brazilian Mato Grosso, then crossed Argentine territory to attack Brazil. Brazil, Argentina and Uruguay signed the Triple Alliance in May 1865. The war dragged on until López was killed at Cerro Corá on 1 March 1870.",
 why="López intervened against Brazil's ally in Uruguay, then invaded Brazilian and Argentine territory, which united his neighbours against him. Brazil and Argentina wanted the Paraguay river open and a weaker Paraguay.",
 then="Paraguay may have lost over half its population, including most of its men; estimates range widely. Brazil and Argentina took border territory, and Brazilian troops occupied Paraguay until 1876.",
 fact="In the last campaigns López's army was made up of boys and old men; at Acosta Ñu in August 1869 Brazilian troops fought a Paraguayan force largely of children, and Paraguay marks the date as Children's Day."),
"la186725": dict(
 bluf="Mexican President Benito Juárez set out to end the French-imposed empire once French Emperor Napoleon III withdrew his troops under US pressure in 1866-67. Republican forces captured Emperor Maximilian at Querétaro in May 1867 and shot him on 19 June despite appeals from European courts. The execution ended the last European attempt to rule Mexico and confirmed the liberal republic and Juárez as the symbol of Mexican sovereignty.",
 now="The execution ended European attempts to rule Mexico and fixed the liberal, secular republic; Juárez, a Zapotec lawyer, became the symbol of Mexican sovereignty that every later government has claimed.",
 story="After the US Civil War the United States pressed France to leave Mexico, and French Emperor Napoleon III withdrew his troops in 1866-67. Emperor Maximilian stayed on. Mexican President Benito Juárez's republican forces besieged him at Querétaro and captured him in May 1867. He was shot on 19 June 1867 with his generals Miguel Miramón and Tomás Mejía.",
 why="Napoleon III pulled out his troops under US pressure and because Prussia's rise demanded them in Europe, which left Maximilian without protection.",
 then="Juárez restored the republic and the liberal reforms that limited the Church's power. He died in office in 1872.",
 fact="Édouard Manet painted the execution as a criticism of Napoleon III, and the painting was banned in France."),
"la187626": dict(
 bluf="Mexican general Porfirio Díaz seized power in 1876 under the slogan 'no re-election' and set out to give Mexico order and growth. He ruled almost continuously until 1911, bringing railways, mines and foreign investment and concentrating land and wealth in a few hands. The inequality and the closed politics of 35 years produced the revolution of 1910.",
 now="The Porfiriato's land concentration and political closure produced the revolution of 1910-20 and the constitution of 1917; 'order and progress' remains the model development dictatorships cite.",
 story="Mexican general Porfirio Díaz led a revolt in 1876 against President Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada's bid for re-election and took the presidency. He ruled from 1876 to 1911 with one four-year break. Railways grew from under 1,000 km to some 19,000 km, mines and foreign investment boomed, and peasants and Indigenous villages lost their land to haciendas.",
 why="Díaz led a revolt against President Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada's bid for re-election. Mexico's elites wanted an end to decades of coups and foreign intervention.",
 then="Peasants lost their land to haciendas and foreign companies. Díaz's rigged re-election of 1910 brought Francisco Madero's call to arms, and Díaz resigned and sailed into exile in May 1911.",
 fact="The line 'Poor Mexico, so far from God and so close to the United States' is attributed to Díaz."),
"la188627": dict(
 bluf="Spain's government ended slavery in Cuba in stages to keep the island, under pressure from the Ten Years' War, in which the enslaved had fought for independence, and from abolitionists at home and abroad. The patronato system of 1880 was abolished in 1886 and the last patrocinados were freed. One of the largest and last slave economies in the Americas ended, and Afro-Cubans' role in the independence wars tied racial freedom to Cuban nationalism.",
 now="Afro-Cuban soldiers' part in the wars of 1868-98, above all under general Antonio Maceo, tied Black freedom to Cuban nationhood, a link that Afro-Cuban music, religion and culture carry at the heart of Cuban identity.",
 story="Cuba's sugar boom had depended on enslaved Africans. In the Ten Years' War of 1868-78 rebels freed enslaved people who joined them. Spain's Moret Law of 1870 freed the newborn and the elderly, the patronato of 1880 turned the rest into bound 'apprentices', and a royal decree of October 1886 ended the system two years early.",
 why="The Ten Years' War, in which rebels freed enslaved people who joined them, made slavery a liability. Abolitionist pressure in Spain and from Britain grew. The end of slavery in the United States in 1865 removed Cuba's last large slaveholding neighbour.",
 then="Formerly enslaved Cubans joined the independence war of 1895-98 in large numbers. Brazil abolished slavery in 1888, the last state in the Americas to do so.",
 fact="Cuba was the second-to-last place in the Americas to abolish slavery; only Brazil, in 1888, came later."),
}
